From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shanshan <samsom0409@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 19 Oct 2026 01:30:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] skip the chunk-span walk when one workspace split holds every row

`UpdateWorkSpaceSplitConfig` finds the group slots a workspace chunk covers by
walking the group list, reading two entries per slot and breaking when the
cumulative row count passes mLimit. When `loopCount == 1` every cumulative
count stays at or below mLimit by construction, so no break condition can
fire, the walk always runs to the end of the table, and its result is fixed:
the chunk starts at row zero, spans every group slot, is the last chunk, and
leaves `rightMatrixExpertNextStartIndex` at the zero
`InitWorkSpaceSplitConfig` gave it.

Writing that result instead of deriving it removes `2 * groupListLen + 1`
global-memory scalar reads from every core. For one TP16 shard of a 256-expert
MoE layer that is 513 reads on each of the 24 cube and 48 vector cores.

Measured on one Ascend910_93 die, A8W4 MSD, K=6144 N=256, 8 active experts of
256 each holding one row, on top of the straight-line path for
`loopCount == 1`. Both forms were built into one binary selected at run time,
so the pair shares a process, a device context, one HBM allocation and one set
of weight banks; six 192.75 MiB banks rotate with the active expert window so
each batch of 32 calls touches 202.1 MB of distinct weight addresses against a
192 MiB L2. The batch is captured into an ACL graph and replayed, because eager
calls to this operator cost as much on the host as the kernel costs on the
device; host cost during replay is 0.86 to 1.70 us per call. Three runs of 40
rounds, arms replayed A,B,B,A per round:

  walk          91.013   90.516   90.412 us per call
  closed form   88.846   88.145   88.724
  saved          2.118    2.414    1.611   (sd 0.227, 0.300, 0.313)

0 positive against 40 negative in each run. Output is bit-identical: the two
forms produce the same int8 codes and the same output scales.

Other loop counts keep the walk, so prefill is unaffected. This reaches
`gate_up` only; the `down` projection of the same layer calls the CANN built-in
`npu_grouped_matmul` and does not run through this operator.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/csrc/gmm/grouped_matmul_swiglu_quant_v2/op_kernel/grouped_matmul_swiglu_quant_v2_a8w4_msd_pipeline.h
+++ b/csrc/gmm/grouped_matmul_swiglu_quant_v2/op_kernel/grouped_matmul_swiglu_quant_v2_a8w4_msd_pipeline.h
@@ -100,7 +100,13 @@
     // batch-one fast path: a single workspace split needs only pre -> mm -> post.
     if (workspaceSplitConfig.loopCount == 1) {
         preProcess.Process(workspaceSplitConfig, 0, pipe);
-        UpdateWorkSpaceSplitConfig(workspaceSplitConfig, 0);
+        // Every cumulative row count stays at or below mLimit here, so no break
+        // condition in UpdateWorkSpaceSplitConfig's walk can fire and its answer
+        // is fixed. rightMatrixExpertNextStartIndex keeps the zero Init gave it.
+        workspaceSplitConfig.leftMatrixStartIndex = 0;
+        workspaceSplitConfig.rightMatrixExpertStartIndex = 0;
+        workspaceSplitConfig.rightMatrixExpertEndIndex = gmmSwigluQuantV2->groupListLen - 1;
+        workspaceSplitConfig.isLastLoop = true;
         if ASCEND_IS_AIV {
             pipe->Reset();
         }
